 ## Abstract
 
-After task failure or a change in requirements, dynamic task graphs can reorganize work, but new generations are easily conflated with code repair, artifact inheritance, and improved acceptance. This study asks what DAG evolution actually accomplishes and when to edit a graph, continue an existing task, add evidence, or wait. A single-deployment mechanism analysis combines 195 Agent Center generation snapshots after explicit fixture exclusion, 169 parent–child transitions, artifact and contract lineages, and 256 synthetic decision responses. Cases show local edits organizing additional validation and delivery obligations, while other defects are repaired within existing tasks; one repair report precedes its corresponding graph edit. Retained graph nodes do not guarantee retained code capabilities, different commits may have identical complete trees, and acceptance under a new contract does not reinstate a withdrawn earlier verdict. Of 169 transitions, 149 retain all parent nodes, describing historical accumulation rather than delivery benefit. In the six valid experimental scenarios, both models score 96/96 across cumulative versus active views and joined versus distributed relations. Under explicit rules and sufficient facts, either representation supports correct decisions. We therefore characterize DAG evolution as adaptation of work organization: graph changes should correspond to changed obligations or dependencies, while artifact inheritance and acceptance validity require separate checks. Net delivery gains from dynamic strategies remain unmeasured, and human verification is outstanding.
+After task failure or a change in requirements, dynamic task graphs can reorganize work, but new generations are easily conflated with code repair, artifact inheritance, and improved acceptance. This study asks which graph changes correspond to new work obligations and how subsequent outcomes can be distinguished from code repairs, added evidence, and changes in acceptance scope. A single-deployment mechanism analysis combines 195 Agent Center generation snapshots after explicit fixture exclusion, 169 parent–child transitions, artifact and contract lineages, and 256 synthetic decision responses. Cases show local edits organizing additional validation and delivery obligations, while other defects are repaired within existing tasks; one repair report precedes its corresponding graph edit. Retained graph nodes do not guarantee retained code capabilities, different commits may have identical complete trees, and acceptance under a new contract does not reinstate a withdrawn earlier verdict. Of 169 transitions, 149 retain all parent nodes, describing historical accumulation rather than delivery benefit. In the six valid experimental scenarios, both models score 96/96 across cumulative versus active views and joined versus distributed relations. All inputs already prescribed the separation of graphs, artifacts, and acceptance. Either representation supported compliance with that supplied protocol; actual evolution strategies were not compared. We therefore characterize DAG evolution as adaptation of work organization: graph changes should correspond to changed obligations or dependencies, while artifact inheritance and acceptance validity require separate checks. Net delivery gains from dynamic strategies remain unmeasured, and human verification is outstanding.
 
 **Keywords:** DAG evolution; agent harness; artifact inheritance; acceptance contract; process reconstruction
 
